    "slug": "ghetto-house",
    "title": "Ghetto House",
    "type": "genre",
    "summary": "Ghetto house arose on Chicago's South and West Sides in the early 1990s, when a younger generation of Black producers sought harder, blunter, cheaper-sounding dance tools than the soulful deep house of the late '80s.",
    "body": "## Ghetto House\n\nGhetto house arose on Chicago's South and West Sides in the early 1990s, when a younger generation of Black producers sought harder, blunter, cheaper-sounding dance tools than the soulful deep house of the late '80s. Working on Roland drum machines and the TB-303, figures like DJ Deeon, DJ Funk, DJ Milton and Jammin Gerald stripped house to its rawest skeleton, looping a relentless 808/909 kick, sparse claps and hi-hats under repetitive, often sexually explicit 'booty' chants. The undisputed home of the style was Dance Mania Records, which flooded Chicago record shops like Gramaphone with cheaply pressed 12-inches built for DJs and house parties rather than radio. The music was functional, communal and unapologetically explicit, soundtracking block parties, skating rinks and teen dance battles across the city's Black neighborhoods. By the late 1990s producers, including DJ Slugo and the Traxmen, accelerated the tempo into 'juke,' roughly 150–165 BPM, with stuttering, syncopated kicks and chopped vocal samples engineered for ferocious competitive dancing. Juke in turn birthed footwork, the hyper-fast, rhythmically dazzling style codified by RP Boo and carried worldwide by DJ Rashad, DJ Spinn and the Teklife crew through labels like Planet Mu and Hyperdub. Often overlooked by house historians who favor its more polished cousins, ghetto house and its descendants represent one of Chicago's most influential and authentically grassroots dance traditions, shaping club, bass and footwork scenes globally.\n\n### Origins\n\n**City:** Chicago  ·  **Country:** USA  ·  **Era:** early 1990s (c. 1992) for ghetto house; late 1990s for juke\n\n### Founders & originators\n\n- **[DJ Deeon](/artist/dj-deeon)** (United States) — Dance Mania figurehead who fused 808/909 minimalism with explicit chants to define ghetto house; tracks like 'Freak Like Me.'\n- **[DJ Funk](/artist/dj-funk)** (United States) — Co-architect of ghetto/booty house and founder of Dance Audio; known for 'Run' and relentless 'booty' tracks.\n- **[Jammin Gerald](/artist/jammin-gerald)** (United States) — Early Dance Mania producer central to the South/West Side ghetto house blueprint.\n- **[Paul Johnson](/artist/paul-johnson)** (United States) — Versatile Chicago producer (later 'Get Get Down') linked to the Dance Mania/Traxmen ghetto-house wave; wheelchair user, died 2021.\n\n### Key venues & labels\n\n`Dance Mania Records` · `Dance Audio` · `Databass Records` · `Juke Trax` · `South and West Side Chicago house parties` · `Gramaphone Records`\n\n### Artists who defined & spread it\n\n- **[DJ Deeon](/artist/dj-deeon)** (United States) — Most defining ghetto house producer; bridged the sound into early juke.\n- **[DJ Funk](/artist/dj-funk)** (United States) — Raw, sample-driven booty tracks made him a national ambassador of the sound.\n- **[DJ Milton](/artist/dj-milton)** (United States) — Prolific Dance Mania producer of stripped, hypnotic tracks.\n- **[DJ Slugo](/artist/dj-slugo)** (United States) — Key transitional figure pushing ghetto house toward faster juke tempos.\n- **[Jammin Gerald](/artist/jammin-gerald)** (United States) — Foundational Dance Mania artist of the early-'90s scene.\n- **[Parris Mitchell](/artist/parris-mitchell)** (United States) — Dance Mania producer behind the 'Project X' style of erotic, minimal tracks.\n- **[Paul Johnson](/artist/paul-johnson)** (United States) — Crossed from ghetto house into pop-house with 'Get Get Down' (1999).\n- **[Traxman](/artist/traxman)** (United States) — Traxmen crew member who carried the sound from ghetto house into footwork.\n- **[RP Boo](/artist/rp-boo)** (United States) — Widely credited as a founding producer of footwork, juke's faster offshoot.\n- **[DJ Rashad](/artist/dj-rashad)** (United States) — Teklife co-founder who took footwork global on Hyperdub; died 2014.\n- **[DJ Spinn](/artist/dj-spinn)** (United States) — Teklife co-leader and Rashad's longtime collaborator.\n- **[Waxmaster](/artist/waxmaster)** (United States) — Dance Mania-era producer/DJ active in the ghetto house and juke transition.\n\n### How they connect\n\n- DJ Deeon built ghetto house using a Roland TR-606 and TB-303 plus explicit lyrics, releasing on Dance Mania, the style's definitive label.\n- Dance Mania (run by Ray Barney/Jesse Saunders origins) released DJ Deeon, DJ Funk, DJ Milton, DJ Slugo, Jammin Gerald, Parris Mitchell and Paul Johnson, the core ghetto-house roster.\n- The Traxmen crew (incl. Traxman, Eric Martin) released the X-rated 'Hit It From the Back,' a primitive bridge toward faster juke rhythms.\n- Ghetto house sped up into juke (c. 150–165 BPM), which producers like DJ Slugo, RP Boo, DJ Rashad and DJ Spinn pushed into footwork.\n- DJ Rashad and DJ Spinn formed the Teklife collective, exporting footwork internationally via the Hyperdub and Planet Mu labels.\n\n### What it influenced\n\nChicago juke · [footwork](/topic/ghetto-house) · UK bassline / niche · baltimore club / jersey club (parallel ghetto-tech lineages) · ghettotech (Detroit, via DJ Assault/DJ Godfather) · contemporary club and bass music",
