    "slug": "tech-house",
    "title": "Tech House",
    "type": "genre",
    "summary": "Tech house grew out of Britain's post-acid-house underground in the early 1990s, fusing techno's clean, machine-built minimalism with the warmth and swing of house.",
    "body": "## Tech House\n\nTech house grew out of Britain's post-acid-house underground in the early 1990s, fusing techno's clean, machine-built minimalism with the warmth and swing of house. Its lineage traces to the 1988 Clink Street RIP parties in London, where Eddie Richards and Mr C (Richard West) spun a rawer alternative to Balearic Shoom. Through the early 1990s those sensibilities matured at warehouse and club nights, above all the Wiggle parties launched by Terry Francis, Nathan Coles and Richards, which became the genre's crucible. According to scene legend, the name itself came from a Mr C mixtape labelled 'Tech' on one side and 'House' on the other; the Croydon shop Swag Records (opened 1993) adopted the term and became a hub, while Mr C's Plink Plonk label and, later, his and Layo Paskin's nightclub The End anchored the sound in London. The music married rugged basslines and steely techno beats to progressive-house grooves while keeping production stripped and functional. A parallel American strand mattered: Detroit and Chicago figures like Stacey Pullen, Derrick Carter and Green Velvet, plus Philadelphia's Josh Wink and West Coast producer Lucas Rodenbush (E.B.E.), pushed techno toward house's body-music feel. Carl Cox carried the hybrid to global main stages. The term lay dormant before Jamie Jones's Hot Creations and the Paradise brand revived and commercialised it in the 2010s, prompting purists to deride the modern, hook-heavy 'tech house' as far removed from its underground roots. The genre's contested origin story (oral, label-driven, transatlantic) remains its defining ambiguity.\n\n### Origins\n\n**City:** London  ·  **Country:** United Kingdom  ·  **Era:** 1993-1997\n\n### Founders & originators\n\n- **[Richard West](/artist/richard-west)** (United Kingdom) — Former Shamen rapper; ran The Drop club night, co-founded Plink Plonk and later The End nightclub; central to coining/popularising the term.\n- **[Eddie Richards](/artist/eddie-richards)** (United Kingdom) — \"Evil Eddie\"; veteran acid-house DJ from the 1988 Clink Street RIP parties, widely called tech house's godfather; co-hosted Wiggle.\n- **[Terry Francis](/artist/terry-francis)** (United Kingdom) — DJ-producer; co-founded the Wiggle parties that crystallised the sound and resident at The End.\n- **[Nathan Coles](/artist/nathan-coles)** (United Kingdom) — DJ-producer and Wiggle co-founder; ran Wiggle and Swag-adjacent labels foundational to the early scene.\n\n### Key venues & labels\n\n`The End (London nightclub, owned by Mr C and Layo Paskin)` · `Wiggle parties (London warehouse/club nights)` · `The Drop club night` · `Swag Records (Croydon shop and label network, opened 1993)` · `Plink Plonk (label co-founded by Mr C and Paul Stone)` · `Eukahouse / Wiggle Records` · `Hot Creations (later wave, Jamie Jones)`\n\n### Artists who defined & spread it\n\n- **[Mr C](/artist/mr-c)** (United Kingdom) — Scene figurehead who linked the rave underground to the term's adoption.\n- **[Terry Francis](/artist/terry-francis)** (United Kingdom) — Defined the rolling, stripped early UK template via Wiggle and Eukahouse.\n- **[Eddie Richards](/artist/eddie-richards)** (United Kingdom) — Bridged 1988 acid house to the 1990s tech-house aesthetic.\n- **[Bushwacka!](/artist/bushwacka)** (United Kingdom) — Plank/Plink Plonk affiliate; Mr C collaborator who broadened the sound.\n- **[Charles Webster](/artist/charles-webster)** (United Kingdom) — Producer noted in early tech-house lineage for warm, machine-driven grooves.\n- **[Derrick Carter](/artist/derrick-carter)** (United States) — Chicago DJ whose jacking, tech-leaning sets fed the transatlantic sound.\n- **[Stacey Pullen](/artist/stacey-pullen)** (United States) — Detroit producer cited as a US techno-house bridge.\n- **[Lucas Rodenbush](/artist/lucas-rodenbush)** (United States) — West Coast innovator releasing tech-house records from c.1995.\n- **[Josh Wink](/artist/josh-wink)** (United States) — Philadelphia producer whose minimal acid-tech crossovers shaped the style.\n- **[Carl Cox](/artist/carl-cox)** (United Kingdom) — Marquee DJ who carried tech house to global main-stage prominence.\n- **[Green Velvet](/artist/green-velvet)** (United States) — Chicago artist (also Cajmere) whose stark, vocal-driven tracks are scene staples.\n- **[Jamie Jones](/artist/jamie-jones)** (United Kingdom) — Hot Creations founder who led the 2010s commercial tech-house revival.\n- **[Dave Angel](/artist/dave-angel)** (United Kingdom) — Producer noted among the UK pioneers fusing techno rigor with house groove.\n- **[Herbert](/artist/herbert)** (United Kingdom) — Experimental sampler associated with the early UK strand.\n\n### How they connect\n\n- Eddie Richards + Mr C DJed together at the 1988 Clink Street RIP parties, the rawer counterpart to Shoom.\n- Wiggle (Francis, Coles, Richards) crystallised the sound and gave it its modern moniker.\n- Legend holds the term came from a Mr C mixtape labelled 'Tech' on one side and 'House' on the other, popularised via Swag Records.\n- Mr C and Layo Paskin opened The End, regarded as tech house's UK home.\n- US techno producers (Carter, Pullen, Wink, Green Velvet) supplied the transatlantic techno-into-house thread.\n- Jamie Jones's Hot Creations re-popularised the term in the 2010s, later derided by purists as commercial.\n\n### What it influenced\n\n[minimal techno crossover](/topic/minimal-house) · 2010s commercial tech house (Hot Creations / Paradise) · Ibiza main-stage house · melodic / progressive house production norms",
