    "slug": "progressive-house",
    "title": "Progressive House",
    "type": "genre",
    "summary": "Progressive house grew out of the United Kingdom's early-1990s club scene as a 'progression' beyond the bright, vocal Italian piano house and American house then dominating dancefloors.",
    "body": "## Progressive House\n\nProgressive house grew out of the United Kingdom's early-1990s club scene as a 'progression' beyond the bright, vocal Italian piano house and American house then dominating dancefloors. Mixmag captured the moment in 1992, describing 'a new breed of hard but tuneful, banging but thoughtful, uplifting and trancey British house.' The sound favored hypnotic builds, evolving basslines, dub-influenced textures and gradual arrangement changes over instant hooks, music designed to unfold across a long DJ set. William Orbit and Dick O'Dell's Guerrilla Records became its early hub, releasing acts like Spooky and React 2 Rhythm, while landmark early tracks, Leftfield's 'Not Forgotten' (1990), Gat Decor's 'Passion' (1992), and work by Underworld and Slam, mapped its DNA. The genre's identity was cemented by two DJs above all: Sasha and John Digweed, whose Renaissance: The Mix Collection (1994) and Northern Exposure (1996) turned the seamless, atmospheric 'journey' into an art form and exported it worldwide, eventually to New York's Twilo. Through the late 1990s and 2000s, brands like Bedrock and Global Underground and DJs such as Hernán Cattáneo, Nick Warren, Sander Kleinenberg and Deep Dish carried a deeper, melodic strain across the globe. Crucially, the term later split: by the late 2000s and early 2010s, 'progressive house' was reappropriated by mainstream EDM (deadmau5, and the big-room melodic builds of Avicii and Swedish House Mafia), describing a louder, anthemic, festival-oriented sound quite distinct from its underground origins. Contested note: many purists insist the original UK progressive house and the later EDM variant share little beyond the name, with Eric Prydz often cited as the figure bridging the two eras.\n\n### Origins\n\n**City:** London  ·  **Country:** United Kingdom  ·  **Era:** c.1990–1994 (term coined 1992; later EDM evolution 2008–2015)\n\n### Founders & originators\n\n- **[William Orbit](/artist/william-orbit)** (United Kingdom) — Co-founded Guerrilla Records, pivotal early progressive house label\n- **[Dick ODell](/artist/dick-odell)** (United Kingdom) — Co-founder of Guerrilla Records with William Orbit\n- **[Sasha](/artist/sasha)** (United Kingdom) — DJ who, with John Digweed, defined and popularized the genre\n- **[John Digweed](/artist/john-digweed)** (United Kingdom) — Sasha's partner in the Northern Exposure/Bedrock progressive sound\n\n### Key venues & labels\n\n`Guerrilla Records (UK)` · `Renaissance (UK club/brand)` · `Bedrock Records (John Digweed)` · `React/Cowboy Records` · `Hooj Choons` · `Renaissance Rooms / Cream / Gatecrasher (UK clubs)` · `Twilo (New York, US)`\n\n### Artists who defined & spread it\n\n- **[Sasha](/artist/sasha)** (United Kingdom) — Foundational DJ; Renaissance and Northern Exposure mixes set the template\n- **[John Digweed](/artist/john-digweed)** (United Kingdom) — Co-architect with Sasha; runs Bedrock label and night\n- **[Leftfield](/artist/leftfield)** (United Kingdom) — 'Not Forgotten' (1990) is an origin-defining early track\n- **[Underworld](/artist/underworld)** (United Kingdom) — Dubnobasswithmyheadman shaped the genre's deeper, hypnotic end\n- **[Gat Decor](/artist/gat-decor)** (United Kingdom) — 'Passion' (1992) a canonical early progressive house anthem\n- **[React 2 Rhythm](/artist/react-2-rhythm)** (United Kingdom) — 'Whatever You Dream' an early Guerrilla-scene exemplar\n- **[Spooky](/artist/spooky)** (United Kingdom) — Guerrilla act ('Don't Panic') central to the early sound\n- **[Slam](/artist/slam)** (United Kingdom) — Soma-affiliated act in the early progressive lineage\n- **[Hernán Cattáneo](/artist/herna-n-catta-neo)** (Argentina) — Carried the deep, melodic progressive sound globally from the 2000s\n- **[Sander Kleinenberg](/artist/sander-kleinenberg)** (Netherlands) — Defined the 2000s European progressive house club sound\n- **[Deep Dish](/artist/deep-dish)** (United States) — Grammy-winning duo who globalized progressive house in the 2000s\n- **[Eric Prydz](/artist/eric-prydz)** (Sweden) — Bridged classic progressive into the modern festival era ('Pjanoo', Pryda)\n- **[deadmau5](/artist/deadmau5)** (Canada) — Central to the 2008–2012 mainstream/EDM progressive house wave\n- **[Nick Warren](/artist/nick-warren)** (United Kingdom) — Way Out West and Global Underground mixes key to the genre's spread\n\n### How they connect\n\n- Guerrilla Records (Orbit/O'Dell) provided the early label home (Spooky, React 2 Rhythm)\n- Sasha & John Digweed's Renaissance (1994) and Northern Exposure (1996) mixes canonized the genre on compilation\n- Shares lineage with UK techno/Italian piano house and the trance scene of the early-mid 1990s\n- Bedrock and Global Underground brands extended the sound internationally (Cattáneo, Warren, Kleinenberg)\n- Eric Prydz and deadmau5 form the bridge to 2010s 'festival' progressive house and mainstream EDM\n\n### What it influenced\n\nTrance and melodic techno crossover · 2000s global club/'GU' DJ culture · Festival/EDM 'big-room' progressive house (Avicii, Swedish House Mafia lineage) · Modern melodic house & techno (Anjunadeep-style sound) · Long-form DJ mix/journey aesthetics",
