    "slug": "bass-house",
    "title": "Bass House",
    "type": "genre",
    "summary": "Bass house is a transatlantic hybrid: it fuses house music's four-on-the-floor pulse with the distorted, sub-heavy basslines of UK garage, 2-step, grime, bassline and dubstep.",
    "body": "## Bass House\n\nBass house is a transatlantic hybrid: it fuses house music's four-on-the-floor pulse with the distorted, sub-heavy basslines of UK garage, 2-step, grime, bassline and dubstep. Its sonic DNA traces to mid-2000s UK fidget and bass-leaning house figures like Switch and Sinden, but the recognisable 'bass house' template crystallised in the early 2010s through London's Jack Beats, whose raw, wet basslines became a touchstone. The style's institutional home, however, emerged in Los Angeles: in January 2014 AC Slater (Aaron Clevenger) launched Night Bass as a monthly party at Hollywood's Sound Nightclub, expressly to platform UK garage, bassline, grime and house sounds he considered under-represented in the US. By June 2015 Night Bass became a label, quickly becoming the closest thing the genre has to a central hub, releasing UK and global artists such as Chris Lorenzo, Taiki Nulight, Petey Clicks and Habstrakt. In parallel, a French axis (Tchami's Confession, Malaa) and an American G-house wave (Wax Motif, Dr. Fresch, Bijou) fed adjacent, often overlapping currents, blurring lines between bass house and G-house. The genre's signature lies in its sound design: vocal-chop growls, talkbox-style mid-range riffs and gut-punch sub-bass dropped over a steady ~125-130 BPM house groove. Beatport formally recognised the rising tide of bass-driven house styles in the mid-2010s. Contested point: 'bass house' is sometimes used loosely as an umbrella for any heavy house, and several originators (Switch, Jack Beats, AC Slater) are credited depending on whether one emphasises sonic invention or scene-building.\n\n### Origins\n\n**City:** London (sound roots) / Los Angeles (scene hub)  ·  **Country:** United Kingdom / United States  ·  **Era:** c. 2012-2016\n\n### Founders & originators\n\n- **[AC Slater](/artist/ac-slater)** (United States) — US producer/DJ who founded Night Bass (party 2014, label 2015), the genre's main hub uniting UK-rooted bass styles.\n- **[Jack Beats](/artist/jack-beats)** (United Kingdom) — London duo (Plus One of Scratch Perverts + Beni G of Mixologists) widely cited as defining the raw, wet bass-house template in the early 2010s.\n- **[Switch](/artist/switch)** (United Kingdom) — Producer often credited, with Sinden, as an early instigator of the heavy fidget/bass-leaning house sound that preceded bass house.\n\n### Key venues & labels\n\n`Night Bass (label/party, LA, founded 2014/2015)` · `Sound Nightclub, Hollywood (original Night Bass home)` · `Confession (Tchami's label, crossover)` · `Dim Mak` · `Mad Decent / Jeffree's` · `Bite This! (Joyryde)` · `Rinse / OWSLA (early Jack Beats outlets)`\n\n### Artists who defined & spread it\n\n- **[AC Slater](/artist/ac-slater)** (United States) — Night Bass founder and figurehead of the modern bass house movement.\n- **[Jack Beats](/artist/jack-beats)** (United Kingdom) — London duo whose distorted basslines defined an early bass-house blueprint.\n- **[Chris Lorenzo](/artist/chris-lorenzo)** (United Kingdom) — Birmingham producer central to the UK bassline-to-bass-house crossover.\n- **[Habstrakt](/artist/habstrakt)** (France) — French producer known for heavy, riff-driven bass house on Night Bass and Never Say Die.\n- **[Joyryde](/artist/joyryde)** (United Kingdom) — UK producer known for aggressive, hip-hop-tinged bass house; runs Bite This!\n- **[Wax Motif](/artist/wax-motif)** (Australia) — Australian producer bridging G-house and bass house.\n- **[Tchami](/artist/tchami)** (France) — French producer whose Confession label pushed the heavier, gospel-tinged side adjacent to bass house.\n- **[Malaa](/artist/malaa)** (France) — Masked French producer associated with the darker G-house/bass-house axis.\n- **[Dr. Fresch](/artist/dr-fresch)** (United States) — LA producer prominent in the G-house/bass-house scene.\n- **[Bijou](/artist/bijou)** (United States) — Arizona producer central to the US G-house/bass-house wave.\n- **[Petey Clicks](/artist/petey-clicks)** (United States) — Early Night Bass roster artist.\n- **[Taiki Nulight](/artist/taiki-nulight)** (United Kingdom) — UK producer key to Night Bass's UK-garage-rooted sound.\n- **[Holly](/artist/holly)** (United Kingdom) — UK DJ/producer in the bass-house and garage crossover scene.\n- **[Sinden](/artist/sinden)** (United Kingdom) — Early instigator of heavy bass-leaning house alongside Switch.\n\n### How they connect\n\n- Directly descends from UK garage, 2-step, grime and UK bassline, importing their swung sub-bass into 4/4 house.\n- Borrows distorted, dubstep/trap-derived bass sound design (talkbox-style growls, mid-range riffs) into a house tempo (~125-130 BPM).\n- Overlaps heavily with G-house (Malaa, Wax Motif, Dr. Fresch) and shares roster/labels.\n- Tchami's future-house circle (Confession) sits adjacent, supplying the heavier crossover edge.\n- Night Bass, run by US-based AC Slater, deliberately platformed under-represented UK genres on US dance floors.\n\n### What it influenced\n\nG-house · modern festival/tech house crossover · UK bassline revival of the late 2010s · speed/bass-driven EDM crossover acts",
